the photographer who took a picture  along the Mexico border that appeared on  the front pages of newspapers around the  world is now sharing the story behind  this picture we tracked down the Reuters  photographer his name is Kim Jung hoon  who took the picture of a mother and her  two daughters running away from a  smoking teargas canister on Sunday near  the us-mexico border he said that he  noticed the kids were barefoot and in  diapers he spoke to Mireya Villarreal  about that moment what was it about them  Kim that struck you what was it about  them that made you take that picture  when I saw the woman and then two girls  I think maybe that can tear a story  because I had a chance to talk to it  many migrants and most of them say they  want better life also they won't give a  better future to their children they say  that's that's one of the region they  want to go to the United States so  that's why I mean in that you know  hectic and chaotic situation maybe just  let me just focus on them Reuters  reporter spoke to a woman yesterday in a  shelter she tried to claim asylum at the  US border on Sunday but was among the  migrants blocked by Mexican police some  rushed the fence prompting the Border  Patrol to fire tear gas Customs and  Border Protection Commissioner Kevin  McKellen and said the gas helps stop and  adults from throwing rocks and he said  it was quote unfortunate that women and  children were affected it's a reminder  that behind every picture is a story  exactly right
 

